{{# Inline SVG icon for use in post markdown, so authors can drop a Phosphor
    icon into a sentence without writing HTML.

    Use:
      {{ comp.icon({ name: "lightbulb", size: 6, color: "esoliaamber" }) }}

    MUST stay on a single line. This tag is emitted into markdown, and the
    markdown-it options in _config.ts set `breaks: true`, which turns every
    newline into a <br>. When this component was written across several lines,
    those <br> landed *inside* the tag and split it apart, so markdown-it no
    longer recognized it as HTML — it rendered the attributes as visible text,
    and `typographer: true` then curled the quotes for good measure. That is
    what readers saw: a literal `<img class=“size-4 …” src=‘…’ inline` in the
    middle of a paragraph, on every icon in every post that used this.

    Keep the attributes on one line and both problems disappear: markdown-it
    parses it as inline HTML, leaves the quotes alone, and Lume's inline plugin
    swaps the whole tag for the SVG at build time.

    `deno fmt` wraps this back onto four lines given the chance, which silently
    reintroduces the bug — so src/_components is excluded from fmt in
    deno.json. Do not remove that exclusion without moving this markup
    somewhere the formatter cannot reach. #}}
<img class="size-{{ size }} fill-{{ color }}-400 inline-block align-[-0.1em] mr-1" src="{{ name |> icon('phosphor', 'duotone') }}" inline>
